    <span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Rentals</span>
    <h1>Yamaha Mio For Beginners Hanoi: Selection Guide</h1>
    <p>The Yamaha Mio is one of the two machines we most often hand to first-time riders in Hanoi, and the reasons are written into its design: a low seat, a light frame and a fully automatic drivetrain. It shares our standard automatic class with the Honda Click at 150,000 VND per day, and it can be collected from our shop at 112 Nguyen Van Cu, Long Bien, any day from 09:00 to 21:00. This guide walks through the selection case for the Mio as a first machine, from ergonomics and controls to rental tiers and the first month of riding. The Mio is small enough to feel manageable on day one and honest enough to keep teaching for a month.</p>
    <p>It sits inside the <a href="/hanoi-scooter-rental/">Hanoi scooter rental</a> hub alongside every other automatic model and guide, and the verified prices for the whole fleet are gathered on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost</a> page. For stays that stretch beyond a few weeks, the <a href="/monthly-motorbike-rental-hanoi/">monthly motorbike rental in Hanoi</a> tier turns daily arithmetic into a single calm number worth checking first.</p>
<nav class="toc"><ol>
<li><a href="#sec-1">Why the Mio suits first-time riders</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec-2">Seat height, weight and balance</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec-3">Learning the twist and go drivetrain</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec-4">Mio versus Honda Click for beginners</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec-5">Storage, comfort and daily ergonomics</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec-6">Rental costs and tier choice</a></li>
